From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: iOS: a non-alertable sleep is a ppsync wait, so a
 system APC reaches it

On desktop, the SIGUSR1 that comes with a system APC ends a sleep in
select() (a non-alertable NtDelayExecution) and runs the APC, such as the
APC_ASYNC_IO that completes a named-pipe read the thread started. On iOS
no signal reaches a thread (wine-unix 0002). The server kicks a thread in
a ppsync wait (wine-unix 0045), but a thread asleep in select() still
waited for its next server wait.

The Rockstar Games Launcher's thread that talks to Rockstar Service sleeps
between its checks of the service's pipe, and on the phone it saw each
reply only at its next 10 s ping. The 500 ms handshake timed out, each
step of a self-update took 10 s or more, and the self-update to build
1311 never finished (PLA-851). Desktop Proton reproduces it once
wineserver drops SIGUSR1 and fsync and esync are off, which leaves the
server waits that a ppsync kick stands for. Delivering SIGUSR1 only to
threads asleep in select() restores the desktop timing: the handshake
answers in 89 ms and the update applies in 1.4 s.

With ppsync, a timed non-alertable sleep is now a ppsync wait on no
object, until the same deadline. A kick ends it, linux_wait_objs() runs
the APC through a zero-timeout server wait, and the sleep goes on. A
sleep forever does the same in a loop. Without ppsync nothing changes.

Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-11-13-rockstar-launcher-self-update.md (PLA-851: Rockstar Service's log on the phone, desktop Proton with SIGUSR1 withheld)
Offered-upstream: no; iOS one-process runtime integration
---
 dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
index 8df2581..69ddc6f 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
@@ -3353,6 +3353,9 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI NtDelayExecution( BOOLEAN alertable, const LARGE_INTEGER *timeou
 
     if (!timeout || timeout->QuadPart == TIMEOUT_INFINITE)  /* sleep forever */
     {
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+        if (inproc_device_fd >= 0) for (;;) linux_wait_objs( inproc_device_fd, 0, NULL, WaitAny, 0, NULL );
+#endif
         for (;;) select( 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL );
     }
     else
@@ -3371,6 +3374,25 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI NtDelayExecution( BOOLEAN alertable, const LARGE_INTEGER *timeou
         status = NtYieldExecution();
         if (!when) return status;
 
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+        /* Playport (PLA-851): on desktop the SIGUSR1 of a system APC ends a sleep in
+         * select() and runs the APC, such as the one that completes a named-pipe read
+         * this thread started. On iOS no signal reaches the thread, and ppsync's kick
+         * reaches only a thread in a ppsync wait, so the APC waited for the sleeper's
+         * next server wait: the Rockstar Games Launcher's thread that polls its
+         * service's pipe between Sleep()s saw each reply only at its 10 s ping. The sleep is
+         * a ppsync wait on no object instead: a kick ends it, linux_wait_objs() runs
+         * the APC through a zero-timeout server wait, and the sleep goes on to the same
+         * deadline. */
+        if (inproc_device_fd >= 0)
+        {
+            LARGE_INTEGER deadline;
+            deadline.QuadPart = when;
+            linux_wait_objs( inproc_device_fd, 0, NULL, WaitAny, 0, &deadline );
+            return STATUS_SUCCESS;
+        }
+#endif
+
         for (;;)
         {
             struct timeval tv;
